WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.270
Next, with just a few lines of configuration,

00:00:03.569 --> 00:00:05.799
you'll add tracing and monitoring to your agent

00:00:05.799 --> 00:00:08.259
so you can spot performance issues in real time.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.990
And you can optimize your workflows with confidence.

00:00:11.333 --> 00:00:12.862
All right, let's go.

00:00:13.132 --> 00:00:14.932
This lesson is all about observability.

00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:17.121
We're going to set up a Phoenix server

00:00:17.121 --> 00:00:19.132
and we're going to watch observability data flow in.

00:00:19.432 --> 00:00:21.233
And we're going to use that

00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:23.332
to identify performance bottlenecks by analyzing traces.

00:00:23.733 --> 00:00:25.832
And we'll use that to optimize tool usage.

00:00:26.405 --> 00:00:28.495
We can even compare workflow performance

00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:30.574
before and after changing our config.

00:00:31.155 --> 00:00:33.075
And by the end of this lesson you should be able to

00:00:33.075 --> 00:00:35.895
understand NAT's unified observation stream.

00:00:36.146 --> 00:00:38.246
What is observability?

00:00:38.246 --> 00:00:41.146
Observability means understanding what's happening

00:00:41.146 --> 00:00:43.646
inside your AI workflows by examining their outputs.

00:00:43.646 --> 00:00:46.945
That's not hard to do at a simple function level.

00:00:47.646 --> 00:00:49.646
The power of NAT is that it enables it

00:00:49.646 --> 00:00:51.646
at all of your functions.

00:00:51.646 --> 00:00:54.945
whether those functions are a complicated sub-agent

00:00:54.945 --> 00:00:58.146
with its own functions that are instrumented for observability

00:00:58.146 --> 00:00:59.945
or a simple Python function.

00:00:59.945 --> 00:01:01.996
It takes all of these and sends

00:01:01.996 --> 00:01:04.046
them to whatever observability server you're using.

00:01:04.646 --> 00:01:08.646
Configuring observability is as simple as modifying your config.

00:01:08.864 --> 00:01:12.265
Here we have a general section at the top of our config

00:01:12.465 --> 00:01:15.165
and we've told it that we are going to be configuring telemetry.

00:01:15.564 --> 00:01:19.064
Specifically, tracing telemetry. And we give it a target.

00:01:19.465 --> 00:01:22.064
We name the target Phoenix and the type is phoenix.

00:01:22.765 --> 00:01:24.465
In this course, we're going to be

00:01:24.465 --> 00:01:26.165
using Phoenix, a UI written by Arize.

00:01:26.527 --> 00:01:29.326
And so we specify that it is a type phoenix,

00:01:29.326 --> 00:01:31.727
and we give it the endpoint to that Phoenix server,

00:01:31.727 --> 00:01:33.546
and we give it an optional project

00:01:33.546 --> 00:01:35.626
name so Phoenix can keep things separated out.

00:01:36.027 --> 00:01:39.626
Phoenix is a handy UI and observability server.

00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:41.819
In this image, we can see

00:01:41.819 --> 00:01:43.865
that someone has run an agentic workflow,

00:01:43.965 --> 00:01:46.165
and we can see trace data from that workflow.

00:01:46.665 --> 00:01:48.844
We can see the LLM calls, the tools the

00:01:48.844 --> 00:01:51.266
agent decided to call, and the results of those tools.

00:01:51.566 --> 00:01:54.865
We can also see the latency that this call took 26 seconds.

00:01:55.159 --> 00:01:57.358
And that's the real power of observability.

00:01:58.058 --> 00:02:00.659
The user might ask which country has the most weather stations

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:02.245
and without observability, we wait a little

00:02:02.245 --> 00:02:04.058
while and we get the correct answer, eventually.

00:02:04.659 --> 00:02:07.459
But with observability, we see that that correct answer came

00:02:07.459 --> 00:02:09.658
with 15 tool calls and 3,000 tokens,

00:02:09.859 --> 00:02:10.920
and it took 8 seconds to

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:12.158
get the answer back to the user.

00:02:12.658 --> 00:02:14.658
That might be something we can optimize later on.

00:02:15.081 --> 00:02:18.181
In this lesson, we're going to be looking at observability and tracing

00:02:18.181 --> 00:02:20.282
in the NeMo Agent Toolkit.

00:02:20.782 --> 00:02:24.181
NAT can emit many different types of observability data,

00:02:24.181 --> 00:02:26.354
OpenTelemetry data or otherwise.

00:02:26.354 --> 00:02:27.830
It's pluggable, so if there's a

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:29.554
type of data that it doesn't output,

00:02:29.653 --> 00:02:32.054
you can write an adapter and plug that in.

00:02:32.854 --> 00:02:36.253
our case, we're going to be emitting OpenTelemetry data in this notebook,

00:02:36.354 --> 00:02:38.061
and we're going to be consuming

00:02:38.061 --> 00:02:40.054
it and looking at it in Phoenix,

00:02:40.153 --> 00:02:43.054
which is a UI written by Arize.

00:02:43.713 --> 00:02:46.313
Normally you would just run phoenix serve in a terminal

00:02:46.313 --> 00:02:48.713
to start Phoenix, but since it's in a notebook,

00:02:48.713 --> 00:02:50.913
we have to run some additional commands.

00:02:51.514 --> 00:02:53.614
Now we have Phoenix running in the background,

00:02:53.614 --> 00:02:57.014
ready to accept our NAT telemetry.

00:02:57.586 --> 00:02:59.640
Before we run NAT and emit data,

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.986
let's take a look at the Phoenix UI.

00:03:02.387 --> 00:03:04.986
This is just some JavaScript here to give you the location

00:03:04.986 --> 00:03:07.486
of Phoenix running on your own system.

00:03:07.586 --> 00:03:09.110
So when you run it, it'll

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:10.887
give you an open Phoenix UI button.

00:03:11.187 --> 00:03:12.680
This is the Phoenix UI and we're

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:14.387
not going to go too deep into it.

00:03:14.486 --> 00:03:17.787
But we can see that it says no traces uploaded yet.

00:03:18.064 --> 00:03:19.726
When our NAT workflow begins to

00:03:19.726 --> 00:03:21.664
send traces, it'll send a project name,

00:03:21.664 --> 00:03:25.264
and that project name will show here along with the traces.

00:03:25.264 --> 00:03:27.264
And if we click on the project,

00:03:27.264 --> 00:03:28.557
we'll be able to see all

00:03:28.557 --> 00:03:30.064
of the traces that have flowed in.

00:03:30.882 --> 00:03:34.181
Let's install our climate_analyzer agent that we've been working on.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:37.621
Now that that's installed, let's look and see what it takes

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.920
to flow OpenTelemetry data from NeMo Agent Toolkit into Phoenix.

00:03:42.305 --> 00:03:45.705
In our configuration file where we have configured our LLMs

00:03:45.705 --> 00:03:49.104
and our workflow and our tools, we can add another section.

00:03:49.104 --> 00:03:51.205
And this section is called general.

00:03:51.805 --> 00:03:55.504
And this is where we can put NAT configuration specific data.

00:03:55.765 --> 00:03:57.765
For instance, telemetry.

00:03:57.966 --> 00:04:00.366
And there's various types of telemetry

00:04:00.566 --> 00:04:02.265
like logging or tracing,

00:04:02.265 --> 00:04:03.966
and we are going to do tracing.

00:04:04.265 --> 00:04:06.566
So we'll have a tracing header here.

00:04:06.665 --> 00:04:09.066
And then we can define many different tracing targets.

00:04:09.277 --> 00:04:12.078
You might have multiple Phoenix servers,

00:04:12.078 --> 00:04:14.078
you might have a Prometheus server.

00:04:14.078 --> 00:04:16.077
In our case, we have one phoenix server

00:04:16.077 --> 00:04:17.778
and we're going to call it phoenix

00:04:17.778 --> 00:04:19.778
and the type is going to be phoenix.

00:04:20.062 --> 00:04:24.362
and we will give this Phoenix configuration an endpoint

00:04:24.562 --> 00:04:27.562
that points to the Phoenix server that we started earlier.

00:04:27.961 --> 00:04:30.862
And we'll give it a project name climate_analyzer_baseline.

00:04:31.449 --> 00:04:34.949
And that's all we need for our NeMo Agent Toolkit workflow

00:04:35.048 --> 00:04:37.548
to start flowing its data into Phoenix.

00:04:38.348 --> 00:04:39.733
In order to see tracing data,

00:04:39.733 --> 00:04:41.348
we need to actually run our agent.

00:04:41.649 --> 00:04:44.548
So let's set up some queries. So we have three queries here,

00:04:44.848 --> 00:04:47.548
the warming rate for Canada, the second coldest year,

00:04:47.548 --> 00:04:49.949
and which country has the most weather stations in our data?

00:04:50.048 --> 00:04:51.649
Let's run these queries.

00:04:51.685 --> 00:04:54.985
And as that's running, I'm going to switch over to Phoenix.

00:04:55.785 --> 00:04:58.685
So now we can see this project climate_analyzer_baseline,

00:04:58.685 --> 00:05:00.551
which is what we called it in

00:05:00.551 --> 00:05:02.685
our config. And we have three total traces,

00:05:02.685 --> 00:05:04.985
which corresponds to the three queries that we ran.

00:05:05.444 --> 00:05:08.144
If we click in here, we can see the trace workflows.

00:05:08.845 --> 00:05:11.144
We have three chains of thought.

00:05:11.845 --> 00:05:13.868
So at a top level, we can see

00:05:13.868 --> 00:05:16.144
some information on these, like the kind of tracing,

00:05:16.845 --> 00:05:18.695
the name of the workflow, which we could

00:05:18.695 --> 00:05:20.545
change in our config if we wanted to.

00:05:21.144 --> 00:05:23.845
The output, any annotations, the start time.

00:05:24.071 --> 00:05:26.670
how many tokens were taken and the latency.

00:05:27.071 --> 00:05:29.571
And it's pretty clear to see that one of these takes

00:05:29.571 --> 00:05:32.071
a lot longer than the other. So let's drill into that.

00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:34.831
Here we can see the thought process

00:05:34.831 --> 00:05:37.071
as the agent is working through its chain.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:40.870
It's making LLM calls, it's making tool calls,

00:05:41.271 --> 00:05:43.571
more LLM calls, more tool calls.

00:05:43.951 --> 00:05:45.705
it makes a lot of LLM

00:05:45.705 --> 00:05:47.752
calls and a lot of tool calls.

00:05:47.851 --> 00:05:49.951
In fact, it looks to me like it's churning.

00:05:50.552 --> 00:05:53.451
After analyzing these calls, we came to the conclusion

00:05:53.552 --> 00:05:55.752
that the agent was looking for station data,

00:05:55.851 --> 00:05:57.190
and we don't have a tool

00:05:57.190 --> 00:05:58.752
for it to specifically get station data.

00:05:58.951 --> 00:06:00.552
So it was going around and around

00:06:00.552 --> 00:06:02.151
trying to collect various bits of information

00:06:02.252 --> 00:06:04.252
from the other tools to look for stations.

00:06:04.935 --> 00:06:07.036
So let's look at our fix.

00:06:07.235 --> 00:06:09.435
This will be in a new config file

00:06:09.735 --> 00:06:12.235
and we will copy our old config file to it

00:06:12.435 --> 00:06:14.735
and in this one we'll add another function

00:06:14.836 --> 00:06:16.836
called station_statistics.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:21.540
and we'll register station_statistics as a new tool in our react_agent workflow.

00:06:22.139 --> 00:06:23.839
Just imagine that everything else is here.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:26.590
The other thing that I'll note that we've changed

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:29.139
is the project name, so when we look at

00:06:29.189 --> 00:06:31.290
Phoenix after we run these queries again,

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:32.889
we'll see a new project there and

00:06:32.889 --> 00:06:34.490
that just helps keep things separated out

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:36.689
so we can see the old calls and the new calls.

00:06:36.990 --> 00:06:38.889
But this will be saved in a new config file.

00:06:39.562 --> 00:06:41.461
And let's run these queries again

00:06:41.461 --> 00:06:43.031
in our updated config file.

00:06:43.512 --> 00:06:45.442
And as those run, we can update Phoenix

00:06:46.091 --> 00:06:48.732
and we can see that they are now flowing in.

00:06:49.322 --> 00:06:51.132
All right, and let's go into our updated project.

00:06:51.708 --> 00:06:54.007
And we can see the three queries that came in,

00:06:54.007 --> 00:06:55.908
but let's look at their timing now.

00:06:56.208 --> 00:06:58.507
And we can see that they all have similar latency,

00:06:58.708 --> 00:07:00.646
which indicates that our agent was

00:07:00.646 --> 00:07:02.908
able to use the new station tool.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:05.750
to drastically reduce the latency in this call.

00:07:06.451 --> 00:07:10.050
Without observability, our agent would be returning the correct information.

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:11.650
But now that we can observe it,

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:13.250
we can reduce the amount of tokens,

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:16.350
the amount of LLM calls, and the latency of the system.

00:07:16.737 --> 00:07:19.237
In this notebook, we have looked at observability,

00:07:19.237 --> 00:07:20.944
how important it is, even though

00:07:20.944 --> 00:07:22.937
we might be getting a correct answer,

00:07:22.937 --> 00:07:25.237
we might be getting it in an inefficient way.

00:07:25.536 --> 00:07:27.937
We've seen how we can use the NeMo Agent Toolkit

00:07:27.937 --> 00:07:30.237
to instrument our agentic workflows

00:07:30.237 --> 00:07:32.737
to flow observability into tools like Phoenix

00:07:32.737 --> 00:07:35.937
and examine a workflow and then fix inefficiencies.
